INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

Concrete. Steel. A wall of dead monitors. One lit.

NADIA KOWALCZYK (30s, hard hat, dust in her braid) crouches at a console. Headset on. A flashlight clamped in her teeth.

She types. Stops. Types.

On the monitor: a progress bar. 71%.

The radio on the desk crackles.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(through static)
Kowalczyk, status.

NADIA
(into headset)
Almost in.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Plant manager's asking. He says the auditors arrive at six.

NADIA
Tell him the auditors can wait.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
He says the auditors can't.

She pulls the flashlight from her mouth. 84%.

NADIA
Then tell him the numbers are wrong. Tell him the silo three weights have been wrong since March.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Repeat?

She says nothing. 96%.

Somewhere below, a heavy door booms shut. Nadia freezes. Kills the flashlight.

Footsteps on the steel stairs. Slow. Unhurried.

The door handle turns.

MARCUS TREVELYAN (50s, plant manager, pressed shirt, no hard hat) steps in. He holds a clipboard. He smiles.

MARCUS
Working late.

NADIA
Just routine.

MARCUS
On a Sunday. At two in the morning.

The radio crackles.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Kowalczyk? You still there?

Marcus crosses to the radio. Switches it off. The room goes silent but for the console fan.

On the monitor: 99%.

Nadia's hand drifts to a wrench on the desk.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Step away from the keyboard, Nadia.

She doesn't.

He drops the clipboard. Lunges.

The wrench swings. The monitor flashes: 100%.
